Z||000004||Fiero Fanatic||01-08-2001||09:02 PM||||I did mine in 3 hours. Put carpet padding down on the rear of the car. Get a 4x4 and chain to secure the engine. Carefully jack the engine to support it so you can disconnect the transaxle and transaxle mounts. Secure the chain then lower the jack down. Then jack the car up until the wheels are off the ground. Disconnect the CV jounts carefully. Pry with a screwdriver. Don't forget to change the drive seals at the CV joints. Push the transaxle to the side to change the bearing, clutch disc, and pressure plate. I forget what it's callled but that thing the flywheel sits on. Don't replace that unless absolutely neccessary. I didn't even f**k with mine, but you'll get one in your new clutch kit. MOST IMPORTANT if your clutch fork is not like new REPLACE IT. I burned up 2 bearings before I replaced the fork. It can be done without dropping the transaxle, exhaust, and the cradle. But remember, it took me 3 times of doing the clutch to perfect this method.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero Fanatic||01-08-2001||09:12 PM||||I'll find my exact documented procedure that I performed. When I formulated this method I wrote down everything I did, then I revised my shop manual. I'll find it then post it here. I was in the same boat. No clutch and No money. Forget the clutch shops. They Suck!<BR>You too can be a fiero clutch pro after this. Just find yourself a good support beam. A chain and bolts to secure it to the engine. Undo the dogbone too when jacking the engine also.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fiero Fanatic||01-08-2001||09:15 PM||||I suppose I'd better ask you first! what year? and what engine? Cause It might not be that useful? I suppose I could still post scanned copies out of my Haines anyway. Check out <A HREF="http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/fiero.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroSTETZ||01-09-2001||02:41 AM||||1987 gt.. thanks for the help! the clutch i finally decided on was the centerforce dual friction. clutch, throwout bearing, and pressure plate came to 320, and they had it IN STOCK! got it from a place in oceanside called "smokey's speed and machine" a most excellent place, actually run by their 82 year old machinist, smokey! what is all the other stuff i should replace while its all apart? i dont have the time/money to replace all my cradle bushings, but i did set aside the money to get an alignment doine after install... i just hope that 3 days is enough time!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero Fanatic||01-09-2001||04:13 AM||||Is that $320? I paid only $100.00 for mine and got a lifetime warranty. Do you have a shuck's auto supply near you. How limited are the auto part stores near you?||||reg||1
