Z||000012||rogergarrison||01-09-2001||12:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero Fanatic:<BR><B>I did mine in 3 hours. Put carpet padding down on the rear of the car. Get a 4x4 and chain to secure the engine. Carefully jack the engine to support it so you can disconnect the transaxle and transaxle mounts. Secure the chain then lower the jack down. Then jack the car up until the wheels are off the ground. Disconnect the CV jounts carefully. Pry with a screwdriver. Don't forget to change the drive seals at the CV joints. Push the transaxle to the side to change the bearing, clutch disc, and pressure plate. I forget what it's callled but that thing the flywheel sits on. Don't replace that unless absolutely neccessary. I didn't even f**k with mine, but you'll get one in your new clutch kit. MOST IMPORTANT if your clutch fork is not like new REPLACE IT. I burned up 2 bearings before I replaced the fork. It can be done without dropping the transaxle, exhaust, and the cradle. But remember, it took me 3 times of doing the clutch to perfect this method.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>check to see if your fork is one of the welded up bits n pieces junk. replace it with the new 1 piece fork. make sure you lube the shaft well when you install the new fork, ive seen em corrode and sieze up. <P>||||reg||1
